Well crossfire - the *other* dnd game I play - has keychains, but it
*is* a regimented scenario - you go into a dungeon and it's the same
every time. So *maybe* the whole idea of set keys binds you to that and
therein lies the problem with nethack. Personally, I'd like to see
different games of nethack with replacements for sokoban, Gehennom (sp?)
etc. And I think the idea of set keys probably eliminates that idea. The
idea of knowing/remembering a keys lock is the way to do it though (more
the former) as how many turns does it take to try 10 keys? *not* 10
turns... 

